one of the most important modern video  games is about to be six years old and  not nearly enough of us are talking  about it  [Music]  since its release warframe has garnered  almost 50 million players it has  frequented steams most played games list  it has launched on every current console  building an avid community across live  streams and social media alike it's even  hosted a yearly convention for dedicated  players by all accounts warframe should  be in the spotlight but it's not in 2013  warframe was a passion project developer  Digital Extremes had just finished years  of contract work and needed to release  an alpha of its next title to stay  afloat the result was a promising albeit  skeletal build of the studio's sci-fi  loot shooter warframe was also  free-to-play at a time when that label  carried a stigma although players and  critics took note as it released on PC  ps4 and Xbox one  the majority including me quickly moved  on Destiny's Preview coverage was  reaching a fever pitch and comparisons  to bungees loot shooter were unfavorable  for the smaller Digital Extremes lastly  it didn't help that warframe was and  still is very complex it's complex in a  good way and I'll get to that but in its  early hours warframe can be overwhelming  many people reach this screen and decide  they're not smart or determined enough  to decipher it  others are uncomfortable with the  general lack of hand-holding feeling  analysis paralysis in the face of so  many ways to play for any of the  preceding reasons warframe never really  built up the critical attention that  Triple A games often do instead it  quietly improved morphing with each new  update partnering with a community that  helped it prosper it added dozens of  characters introduced to new open worlds  release major story base quests as the  devs often say warframe carved out its  own little pocket in the loot shooter  space the result is one of the more  intriguing phenomenons in modern  videogames a massive successful thriving  title that sites like GameSpot rarely  talked about with so many developers  entering the games as service space and  even more going free-to-play warframe is  now more relevant than ever more  developers should learn from it and if  you ask me we should all be playing it  [Music]  full disclosure I was super late to the  warframe party for years I viewed it  from a distance seeing it as just  another Luke Rhine  too complex to grasp when so many other  games were upfront with their ideas but  three months ago over the holidays I  dove in I've since logged 170 hours I've  collected new characters built better  weapons upgraded hundreds of mods I've  traveled one of the more rewarding  learning curves I can recall and I still  have a ton to learn if you've tried  describing warframe to someone you might  have found it difficult yes it's a  sci-fi action PvE game where you play as  space ninjas in a variety of mission  types but as a loot based game it breaks  the mould titles such as anthem destiny  Diablo and borderlands rely on frequent  trickles of weapon drops from dead  enemies you fight better opponents to  gain better loot allowing you to fight  even better opponents for even better  loot warframe is a grind as well but in  many ways it puts you in control of the  grind you don't gather new equipment in  the field instead you gather the  materials to build the weapon gear or  character you want furthermore you are  in charge of the mods or elemental  effects that would already be fixed to  items in other games in warframe you  have much more say over how your weapons  function than you would in Diablo for  example where you have to respec items  with a dice roll to get a build that you  like it's a shame that the mod system  seems so complicated at first blush  because it's actually one of the more  intriguing parts of warframe it lets you  turn your bolt or assault rifle into a  critical hit factory on one mission then  swap in new mods for tons of radiation  damage on the next there are countless  combinations of enemies mission types  and tile sets and learning to mod your  equipment for each prevents warframe  from ever getting stale I'm reminded of  all things of the matrix the more I  learn warframes mod system and all of  its systems really the more powerful I  feel what is he doing he's beginning to  believe gaining more power in loot based  games is nothing new but warframe  doesn't automatically increase numbers  for you it teaches you how to do it  yourself  of course like neo you can also dodge  bullets and of course like neo  eventually you won't have to  what I'm saying is that despite the  presence of RNG item farming and some  archaic free-to-play tedium warframes  grind as nuanced warframes grind is fun  to learn by handing the reins of the  power fantasy over to you Digital  Extremes  acknowledges that you are an intelligent  player capable of dictating your own  definition of improvement  [Music]  this is mark rosewater he's head  designer of Magic the Gathering and has  been since 2003 even if you're not into  magic or for that matter collectible  card games mark is one of the more  insightful people when it comes to game  design in general a few years ago he  coined the term lenticular design it's  taken from lenticular images those  pictures that change depending on the  angle you see them from mark use this to  describe systems that are simultaneously  simple to the beginner but more advanced  to the experienced player I know that  sounds like a paradox but bear with me  because it's actually at the heart of  many great modern games take overwatch  for example many of its characters are  lenticular heroes my favorite example is  tracer - the beginning player she has  teleportation powers that make her very  hard to hit - the veteran she's a great  skirmisher someone who can pest her  enemy groups with little intention of  actually killing them she can buy time  for the rest of her team tracer is  lenticular because in this case neither  the rookie nor the veteran are wrong  both philosophies are viable in their  own way it's only through time and  experience that you'll see her from a  different albeit more useful angle  warframes characters or frames are a  prime example of this philosophy each of  them has an obvious theme from embers  fire attacks - mags use of magnetic  abilities then there's rhino a frame  that relies on tanking and brute force  those are all these surface level  attributes and likely the factors that  will help new players decide which  frames to use these are also the  characteristics that make experimenting  with each of the current 39 characters  so fun veteran players however will see  the nuance in their ninjas take mags  polarizability in earlier missions it's  an effective way to do decent crowd  damage it's obvious it's satisfying and  new players will just get it if you use  the right mods though polarize becomes  an extremely useful power in high-level  missions it damages shielded enemies and  reduces the protection of armoured  Marines  it also replenishes the shields of any  allies to touch it  here's another example this is Frost a  warframe based around ice powers the  last ability he learns is called  avalanche which first freezes then  shatters enemies in close proximity to  the beginner this is his most attractive  ability and they wouldn't be wrong to  use it against enemy groups season  warframe players however will know that  in the late game frost is best used as a  defensive character specifically with  his snow globe ability it creates a  protective dome around the frame and his  allies and becomes a major asset in  defense missions and difficult boss  fights I could go on for hours about  more of this multifaceted character  design but I really don't want to spoil  the discoveries you'll have on your own  the last thing I'll say is that the  excitement of this lenticular depth  doesn't wear off in the later hours in  fact it's somehow the opposite Digital  Extremes has this talent for making  warframe more intriguing the more you  play you'll get to the point where you  have a 10 character rotation and a  variety of loadouts for each mission  type it feels like the game is rewiring  your brain with each new scenario it  throws at you this dependence on  colorful heroes with hidden depth is  becoming more prevalent by the year  successful games such as Rainbow six  siege apex legends and overwatch are all  proof of that and if developers haven't  already looked to warframe for  inspiration they should it's a veritable  iceberg of lenticular design and a  shining example of deceptive simplicity  this isn't to say warframe is not flawed  though in fact I wish the devs would  extend this two-sided design to other  areas as well as I said before the  complexity and learning are a huge part  of the games charm but sometimes that  complexity is less of a funnel and more  of a wall about five percent of my time  with warframe hasn't actually involved  playing the game it's consisted of  skimming through wiki's and forums some  objectives are too vague some crafting  materials too hidden for me to call it  clever  sometimes it feels as if warframe uses  complexity just because it can now I'm  not saying Digital Extremes should  explain each and every little thing to  the player but I do think the player  base would benefit if this  studio found ways to ease newcomers into  a few more of its intricacies a more  hands-on modding tutorial for example or  a simple prompt saying focus on clearing  the star chart most of the game will  unfold from there these would do wonders  for rookie players and more players are  only going to benefit everyone involved  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  it's telling that it's taken me this  long to address the free-to-play  elephant in the room  I rarely felt the need to spend real  money and when I did I didn't regret it  forgive my hyperbole here but more than  any game I played warframe feels like a  partnership between the developers and  me I was happy to give them money I was  happy to support the studio in this  ongoing endeavor there's an example  highlighted in no clips documentary that  perfectly sums up Digital Extremes views  on monetization when the studio released  koo bros the game's canine combat  companions it allowed you to pay real  money for randomized fur patterns and  appearances upon seeing that one player  had pulled that metaphorical lever over  200 times the team realized that had  created a slot machine they promptly  pulled the feature to prevent further  excessive gambling  so in essence Digital Extremes pulled a  micro transaction feature because one  player had used it too many times  Digital Extremes has done so many things  it did not need to do it added to open  worlds to a game that previously relied  on rapid fire missions  it allowed talented community artists to  sell their own armor sets during the  week I began to write the script the  team released night wave a series of  daily and weekly challenges framed as an  in-game radio show there's even a twist  in the mid game hours that  single-handedly changed my perception of  what exactly warframe is but the  community is surprisingly great about  not spoiling this so I'll honor that  here and that's as good an example as  any to character as warframes community  like any group dedicated to something  they love it has its occasional sore  spots but get good mentality in  particular rears its ugly head in the  late game hours but take one trip to the  warframe forums ask for help through the  in-game chat jump into Digital Extremes  dev stream a bi-weekly show that's  coming up on its one hundred and twenty  sixth episode at each and every turn  warframe emphasizes cooperation between  players developers Tenno Jen artists and  YouTube creators alike the theme song  for the recent Fortuna update sums this  up perfectly we all lift  gather  [Music]  we're frame as many things it's a case  study for community involvement  it's a paragon for free-to-play  standards it's a master class in  multi-faceted design and now at the end  of my video  there are countless things I wish I  could have touched on but I'll end by  saying this there is no wrong time to  start playing warframe in fact I would  say now is the best time with more  studios joining the free-to-play scene  and even more seeking balance in their  progression systems warframe is the game  we should be looking to it began as a  product of what games were but has since  become an example of what games can be  and its age doesn't make it antiquated  or obsolete warframes  age makes it a monument and it's time we  all started paying attention

